Three independent, pre-existing mismatches made the signal generation
pipeline unusable as originally wired:

1. calculate_indicators() (used by main.py/backtesting/backtest.py)
   produces EMA50/EMA200/RSI/ATR; FeatureEngine.analyze() needs
   EMA_50/EMA_200/RSI_14/ATR_14.
2. FeatureEngine.analyze() never sets a 'regime' key on the
   intelligence dict it builds. SignalEngine.generate() branches
   almost entirely on that key — without it, every signal fell
   through to WAIT regardless of trend/momentum. This pipeline could
   never have produced a BUY or SELL signal as originally wired.
3. SignalEngine returns {'action': ...}; risk_controller.approve_trade
   and execution.simulator.simulate_trade both expect
   {'signal': ...}.

generate_trading_signal() bridges all three: column aliasing, regime
injection via the already-tested core.regime.detect_regime (reusing
existing classification logic rather than inventing new regime rules),
and an action->signal value mapping. Calls FeatureEngine/SignalEngine/
SignalScorer directly rather than through
core.decision_pipeline.DecisionPipeline, whose own 'scanner
intelligence' branch expects yet another different confidence key
(market_score) that would silently zero out confidence — see
docs/architecture.md for the full trace.

8 tests covering each regime/trend/momentum branch, the ATR-column
precondition, explicit regime override, and the signal-value contract
guarantee.
